				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "# TT Namespacing — a poor-man's namespace convention for Smalltalk\n\n## The idea\n\nA class can **know** that its name contains a namespace prefix and a\nlocal name, and can choose to render either. Three traits carry the\nconvention, all opt-in:\n\n- **`TNamespaced`** — derives the namespace prefix from a `ˆ` baked into\n  the class name itself.\n- **`T2Namespace`** — fixed 2-character prefix (e.g. `Gt` for `GtImage`).\n- **`T3Namespace`** — fixed 3-character prefix (e.g. `Gt4` for `Gt4Foo`).\n\nAny class can mix in one of these to become a namespaced class. The\nnamespace lives **in the class's behaviour**, not in an external registry.\n\n## Protocol\n\nClass-side methods (where namespace info lives):\n\n- `ttPrefixSize` — number of leading characters in the class name that\n  form the namespace prefix.\n- `ttNamespace` — the namespace prefix as a Symbol.\n- `ttLocalName` — the class name with the prefix removed.\n- `ttFullName` — the full class name as a Symbol.\n- `isNamespaced` — `true`.\n\nInstance-side methods (for printing and labelling):\n\n- `ttDisplayName` — defaults to `ttLocalName`.\n- `ttFullName` — delegates to the class.\n- `isNamespaced` — delegates to the class.\n- `printOn:` — prints as `'a <localName>'` instead of `'a <fullName>'`.\n\n## Usage\n\n```code\nGT4 image uses: T2Namespace.\n\"GT4 image's namespace is Gt, local name Image.\"\n\nGt4Foo uses: T3Namespace.\n\"namespace Gt4, local name Foo.\"\n\nTTˆObject uses: TNamespaced.\n\"namespace TT, local name Object — derived from the ˆ in the name.\"\n\nObject uses: T2Namespace.   \"namespace Ob, local name ject — opt-in only\"\n```\n\n## Discovery\n\n```code\nTheHub class >> allNamespaces [\n    ^ (Smalltalk allClasses\n        reject: [ :c | c isTrait ]\n        thenCollect: [ :c | c askFor: #ttNamespace ])\n        reject: #not\n        as: Set\n]\n```\n\nEach class answers `ttNamespace` if it has a namespacing trait; `askFor:`\nreturns `false` for classes that don't, so the registry *is* the image —\nno separate metadata to maintain.\n\n## Why this is new\n\nNo existing Smalltalk convention lets a class advertise its own name with\noptional namespace rendering. Class names are flat Symbols; the namespace\ninformation (if any) is external, in a registry, in a package, in a prefix.\n\nWith this convention, the namespace is part of the class's behaviour. A\nclass that mixes in `TNamespaced` *is* a namespaced class, automatically.\n\n## Properties\n\n1. **Identity is honest.** The class *is* what its name says. No\n   aliasing, no canonicalisation.\n2. **Rendering is configurable.** Debug print can show either the full\n   or the local name; the choice is policy, not hard-coded.\n3. **The convention is enforced by behaviour.** Any class mixing in a\n   `T*Namespace` trait is namespaced. No external registry to keep in\n   sync.\n4. **Reflection still works.** `self class name` returns the full name;\n   `self ttDisplayName` returns the local name; both are methods, both\n   can be overridden.\n5. **Backward-compatible.** Classes that don't opt in are unchanged.\n   `isNamespaced` returns `false` for them.\n6. **Composable.** The `tt`-prefixed names avoid clashes with `Object\n   >> #namespace`, `#fullName`, `#localName`, etc. that already exist\n   in Pharo.\n7. **No rename required.** Every existing class can opt in by adding a\n   trait. `Object`, `Dictionary`, `Behavior` — all namespaced\n   retroactively.\n\n## What to add to make it complete\n\n### A registry or search path\n\n```code\nTheHub class >> ensureClass: aString ifAbsent: aBlock [\n    \"Resolves 'ˆObject' by walking the registered namespaces.\n     Resolves 'TTˆObject' directly. Returns aBlock value if not found.\"\n    aString size = 0 ifTrue: [ ^ aBlock value ].\n\n    aString first = $ˆ ifFalse: [\n        \"qualified name: namespaceˆlocal\"\n        ^ self resolveQualified: aString ifAbsent: aBlock ].\n\n    \"unqualified: scan the active namespace search path\"\n    ^ self resolveUnqualified: aString allButFirst ifAbsent: aBlock\n]\n```\n\n### A naming policy\n\nWhen you create a new class in a namespace, do you auto-prefix `ˆ`, or\ndo you write `TTˆObject` manually?\n\n- **Auto-prefixing** is harder to implement but cleaner to use. Requires\n  intercepting class creation in the namespace.\n- **Manual** is what your code already does, and matches the Smalltalk\n  tradition of explicit naming.\n\nEither is consistent with the design.\n\n## Sharp edge: ambiguity\n\nIf two namespaces both define `ˆFoo`, and you look up `'ˆFoo'`, which\nwins? Recommended policy:\n\n- **No implicit resolution.** `'ˆFoo'` only works if *exactly one*\n  namespace is in scope.\n- **Explicit qualification:** `'TTˆFoo'` always works.\n- **Ambiguity is an error**, not a silent pick.\n## Verdict\n\nA coherent, small, principled design. The namespace lives in the class's\nbehaviour (via traits), the discovery walks the image, and rendering is\nconfigurable per-instance. The price is paid in:\n\n- **Tool support**: browsers, refactoring tools, and code search show\n  the full class name (with the namespace prefix and/or `ˆ`). They\n  don't know about the trait protocol, so `ttDisplayName` only renders\n  when the receiver is explicitly printed.\n- **Onboarding**: a one-paragraph note explaining the trait family is\n  required for any new contributor.\n- **Encoding** (only for `TNamespaced`): the `ˆ` must be the\n  precomposed form (U+02C6) and survive NFC normalisation across\n  operating systems.\n\nThe price is **not** paid in correctness: class identity, reflection,\nand tool round-trip all work.\n"
			}
		]
	},
